# Shell cwd hygiene

The Shell tool keeps **one persistent shell** whose cwd survives between
calls. If that directory stops existing, every later spawn dies before the
command runs, and the tool reports `no exit status` — which reads like a hang
but is not one.

## The error lies to you

The terminal file shows:

```
cwd: "/private/tmp/some-dir-you-deleted"
error: "spawn /path/to/zsh ENOENT"
```

`ENOENT` names the **shell binary**, so it looks like zsh is missing. It
almost never is — `spawn` reports `ENOENT` for a missing *cwd* too. Three
prior sessions (2026-06-10, 2026-07-02, 2026-08-04) each burned several turns
verifying the binary before finding the real cause. Check the `cwd:` line
first; only then check the binary.

## Recovery

On the **first** `no exit status`, don't retry blindly:

1. Read the terminal file for this shell (`terminals/<id>.txt`) and look at
   `cwd:` and `error:`.
2. If `ENOENT`, re-issue the same command with `working_directory` pinned to
   the workspace root from your session context. That alone fixes it — no
   session restart, no user intervention.

## Prevention

- **Never delete the directory the shell is sitting in.** Both `rm -rf` and
  `git worktree remove` have caused this — the latter when an automated
  land/integration flow removed a worktree the shell still sat in. On
  2026-08-04 it was an `rm -rf` of a `/tmp` scratch dir the shell had `cd`'d
  into.
- **Don't `cd` into scratch or worktree dirs at all.** Run throwaway scripts
  by absolute path, or pass `working_directory` per call, so the persistent
  shell never sits anywhere disposable.
- Before any command that removes a directory, confirm it is not the cwd or
  an ancestor of it. This complements the `rm` safety gate: that rule guards
  *what* you delete, this one guards *where you are standing* when you do.
